{"conjecture": "Long-running systems degrade because each locally correct change accretes a new abstraction layer that translates between the evolved domain model and the now-fossilized code model, and these layers compound multiplicatively rather than additively. The cognitive cost of a future change is therefore proportional to the product of semantic distances across all intervening layers, not the distance between the current domain concept and the target code. This explains why systems become harder to change even when no single change was wrong: the drift is an emergent property of layer accretion itself, invisible to any individual review."}
